  "notes": "Anchors ranked and measured in docs/scraper-inventory/whatsapp.md. Candidates are ordered best-first; the runtime records which index matched, and a rising index is the earliest warning of a client redesign. Row classes are hashed and are deliberately absent from every candidate list. Where a call site used a comma-joined selector, candidate 0 is that exact same string: a comma-joined querySelector returns the first match in DOCUMENT order, which is not the same node as first-candidate-in-list order, so keeping it verbatim is what makes the migration behaviour-identical. One selector was dropped rather than carried over: 'div._ak8k span', the sixth and last fallback of the row-subtitle list, which anchored on a hashed build-artifact class from a WhatsApp build that no longer exists. It was unreachable behind five stable candidates and could only ever have produced a wrong match. Pulling it out of a concatenated string is exactly the kind of thing this manifest exists to make visible.",
